{
  "title": "Count inversions",
  "lesson": "dsa/07-sorting",
  "kind": "program",
  "lang": "any",
  "cases": [
    {
      "name": "the example",
      "stdin": "5\n3 1 4 5 2\n",
      "stdout": "4\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "already sorted",
      "stdin": "4\n1 2 3 4\n",
      "stdout": "0\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "reversed",
      "stdin": "4\n4 3 2 1\n",
      "stdout": "6\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "one number",
      "stdin": "1\n7\n",
      "stdout": "0\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "equal numbers are not inversions",
      "stdin": "4\n2 2 2 2\n",
      "stdout": "0\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "with duplicates",
      "stdin": "5\n2 3 2 1 2\n",
      "stdout": "5\n"
    },
    {
      "name": "100,000 numbers (needs O(n log n))",
      "stdin_py": "'100000\\n' + ' '.join(str((i * 7919 + 13) % 100003) for i in range(100000)) + '\\n'",
      "stdout": "2499927115\n"
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Write merge sort first and make sure it sorts correctly.",
    "In the merge step, when you take an element from the right half, it is smaller than everything still waiting in the left half. Add that many to the count.",
    "Take from the left half when the two front elements are equal, so that equal values are not counted."
  ]
}
